{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"move-semantics","title":"Move Semantics & rvalue References","group":"Copy, Move & Smart Pointers","order":33,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Moving transfers a resource from an object that's about to die (or that you no longer need) instead of copying it. A <code>std::vector</code> move is three pointer copies — no matter how many elements it holds.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right-left\"></i>Copy vs move</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; a(1'000'000, 7);\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; b = a;               // copy: allocates and copies a million ints\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; c = std::move(a);    // move: steals a's buffer\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // a is now empty (valid, unspecified)\n\n    std::string s = \"a fairly long string that will not fit in SSO\";\n    std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; names;\n    names.push_back(s);                   // copies\n    names.push_back(std::move(s));        // moves — s is left valid but unspecified\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; names.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; names[1].size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0 1000000 1000000\n2 45</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Writing move operations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nclass Buffer {\n    int* data_ = nullptr;\n    std::size_t size_ = 0;\npublic:\n    explicit Buffer(std::size_t n) : data_(new int[n]{}), size_(n) {}\n    ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; }\n\n    Buffer(const Buffer&amp; o) : data_(new int[o.size_]), size_(o.size_) {\n        std::copy(o.data_, o.data_ + size_, data_);\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"copy\\n\";\n    }\n    Buffer(Buffer&amp;&amp; o) noexcept                                        // move constructor\n        : data_(std::exchange(o.data_, nullptr)), size_(std::exchange(o.size_, 0)) {\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"move\\n\";\n    }\n    Buffer&amp; operator=(Buffer&amp;&amp; o) noexcept {                           // move assignment\n        if (this != &amp;o) {\n            delete[] data_;\n            data_ = std::exchange(o.data_, nullptr);\n            size_ = std::exchange(o.size_, 0);\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n    Buffer&amp; operator=(const Buffer&amp;) = delete;   // kept short for the example\n    std::size_t size() const { return size_; }\n};\n\nBuffer make() { return Buffer(8); }             // guaranteed elision: no copy, no move\n\nint main() {\n    Buffer a(4);\n    Buffer b = std::move(a);        // move\n    Buffer c = b;                   // copy\n    Buffer d = make();              // nothing printed\n    d = std::move(c);               // move assignment\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; d.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>move\ncopy\n0 4 0 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Steal</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Take the source's pointers/handles.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Reset the source</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Leave it in a valid, destructible state (usually empty) — <code>std::exchange(o.p, nullptr)</code> does both in one step.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Mark <code>noexcept</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>std::vector</code> only moves elements during reallocation if the move constructor is <code>noexcept</code>; otherwise it copies to keep the strong guarantee.</div></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i>Why <code>noexcept</code> matters</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nstruct Safe   { Safe() = default;   Safe(const Safe&amp;)   { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"C\"; } Safe(Safe&amp;&amp;) noexcept { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"M\"; } };\nstruct Unsafe { Unsafe() = default; Unsafe(const Unsafe&amp;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"C\"; } Unsafe(Unsafe&amp;&amp;)       { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"M\"; } };\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;Safe&gt; a(2);\n    a.reserve(10);             // reallocation moves the 2 elements\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::vector&lt;Unsafe&gt; b(2);\n    b.reserve(10);             // reallocation copies — the move might throw\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>MM\nCC</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pitfalls</h3>\n<ul><li><code>std::move</code> only <em>casts</em>; if the type has no move constructor, the copy constructor is used.</li><li>Don't use a moved-from object except to assign to it or destroy it (standard types guarantee “valid but unspecified”).</li><li><code>return std::move(local);</code> <strong>prevents</strong> copy elision — just <code>return local;</code> (it's moved automatically if not elided).</li><li><code>const</code> objects can't be moved from: <code>std::move(constObj)</code> yields <code>const T&amp;&amp;</code>, which binds to the copy constructor.</li></ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is an rvalue reference?</summary><div><p>A reference declared with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> that binds only to rvalues (temporaries or objects cast with <code>std::move</code>). It lets a function overload specifically for “this object is expiring”, so it can steal resources instead of copying. Inside the function, a named rvalue reference is itself an lvalue.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why should move constructors be noexcept?</summary><div><p>Containers like <code>std::vector</code> promise the strong exception guarantee when growing. They can only move elements into the new buffer if moving can't throw (otherwise a half-moved vector couldn't be restored), so they check <code>std::is_nothrow_move_constructible</code> and fall back to copying.</p></div></details></section>"}